<BaseLayout title="Terms and References" description="Plain-English definitions of terms used in the homelab and engineering documentation.">
  <section class="section"><div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">Engineering Lab · Reference</p>
    <h1>Terms and References</h1>
    <p class="lead">Plain-English definitions for the technologies in the homelab. See the <a href="/building-blocks/">Building Blocks</a> page for how each component fits into our environment.</p>
    <article id="ansible" class="term"><h2>Ansible</h2><p><strong>What it is:</strong> Agentless automation for configuring servers and deploying repeatable infrastructure changes using inventories, playbooks and roles.</p><p><strong>Why it matters here:</strong> We run Ansible from <code>admin-01</code> to deploy and audit the managed Linux estate, including Alloy. Read-only checks compare installed configurations with what Loki actually receives.</p><p><strong>Practical lesson:</strong> Ansible templates command arguments with Jinja2 before running them remotely. Docker format strings containing double curly braces can therefore trigger an Ansible template error. Prefer Docker JSON output parsed with Python for these ad-hoc audits.</p></article>
    <article id="jinja2" class="term"><h2>Jinja2</h2><p><strong>What it is:</strong> A template language used by Ansible to substitute variables and conditionally render configuration files and task arguments.</p><p><strong>Why it matters here:</strong> Ansible renders Alloy configuration templates and also processes ad-hoc shell arguments. Docker's own Go-template syntax, such as <code>{{'{{.Name}}'}}</code>, can conflict with Jinja2. The resulting template error happens before the remote Docker command runs.</p><p><strong>Where:</strong> Ansible controller and IaC templates, not a standalone production service.</p></article>
    <article id="docker" class="term"><h2>Docker</h2><p><strong>What it is:</strong> A container runtime for packaging and running applications with their dependencies.</p><p><strong>Why it matters here:</strong> Greenbone and Komodo have observed container log streams in Loki; the separate BirdNET-Go host still needs its Docker application-log coverage assessed. Alloy can discover containers and collect their logs when explicitly enabled.</p><p><strong>Important distinction:</strong> An active container does not guarantee that its logs reach Loki. Check its logging driver, Alloy discovery and actual Loki stream.</p></article>
    <article id="json" class="term"><h2>JSON (JavaScript Object Notation)</h2><p><strong>What it is:</strong> A human-readable structured data format using objects, arrays, strings, numbers and other basic values.</p><p><strong>Why it matters here:</strong> Docker inspection, the estate inventory and monitoring APIs expose structured JSON. Scripts can parse fields directly rather than depending on fragile text matching or conflicting command-template syntax.</p><p><strong>Important distinction:</strong> JSON is a format, not encryption; avoid publishing credentials or raw sensitive log contents.</p></article>
    <article id="python" class="term"><h2>Python</h2><p><strong>What it is:</strong> A general-purpose programming language used for automation, parsing and testing.</p><p><strong>Why it matters here:</strong> Our read-only Loki audit parses API JSON and reports source labels and timestamps without printing log lines. The isolated Pi-hole privacy test uses Python with Snappy and Protobuf to inspect synthetic outbound log batches.</p><p><strong>Where:</strong> Administrative scripts and isolated test environments; installing a test dependency does not create a new production service.</p></article>
    <article id="snappy" class="term"><h2>Snappy</h2><p><strong>What it is:</strong> A fast, lossless compression format. Lossless means decompression recovers the original bytes exactly.</p><p><strong>Why it matters here:</strong> Alloy sends compressed log batches to Loki. The isolated Pi-hole privacy test uses <code>python-snappy</code> to decompress a synthetic Loki request so we can inspect what would be forwarded.</p><p><strong>Important distinction:</strong> Compression does not anonymise or encrypt data. The privacy filter must remove domains and client addresses <em>before</em> the message is compressed.</p><p><strong>Where:</strong> Installed in the Ansible-managed, isolated test environment on <code>dns-01</code>; not a separate production service.</p></article>
    <article id="protobuf" class="term"><h2>Protocol Buffers (Protobuf)</h2><p><strong>What it is:</strong> A compact binary format for representing structured data. It is often used to exchange messages between services.</p><p><strong>Why it matters here:</strong> Loki push requests contain structured log streams and entries. After Snappy decompression, our synthetic receiver decodes the Protobuf message to inspect the individual outgoing log lines.</p><p><strong>Important distinction:</strong> Protobuf defines how data is encoded; it does not itself filter, secure or anonymise log contents.</p><p><strong>Where:</strong> The <code>protobuf</code> Python package is installed alongside <code>python-snappy</code> in the isolated <code>dns-01</code> test environment.</p></article>
    <article class="term"><h2>How they work together</h2><p>Pi-hole synthetic log → Alloy privacy filter → Loki Protobuf message → Snappy compression → local test receiver → Snappy decompression → Protobuf decoding → inspect allowed event names and reject leaked identifiers.</p><p><strong>Status:</strong> The decoding dependencies are installed. The isolated end-to-end privacy test passed, and both DNS resolvers now forward sanitised Pi-hole event tokens. Recent production samples of 100 entries per resolver contained no unexpected lines; this is sampled evidence, not an exhaustive historical privacy guarantee.</p><p>Deployment and verification instructions are maintained in the private homelab-platform IaC repository.</p></article>
